WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.920
Disease costs humanity three hundred and ninety-seven point four trillion dollars annually.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.740
That's more than global G.D.P. (one hundred and one trillion dollars).

3
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.720
The number sounds wrong because economists spent centuries measuring productivity without accounting for the people who

4
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:25.860
stopped being productive on account of being dead. The missing piece is disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s (Disability-Adjusted

5
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.820
Life Years): healthy years deleted by disease. Die at forty instead of eighty?

6
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.300
That's forty D.A.L.Y.s. Spend ten years severely depressed?

7
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.220
That's five D.A.L.Y.s. Globally: two point five five billion D.A.L.Y.s annually.

8
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:48.820
At three times G.D.P. per capita (one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per year), that's three hundred and eighty-two

9
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:57.920
point five trillion dollars in lost human potential, plus eight point two trillion dollars in direct medical costs and six

10
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.460
point seven trillion dollars in lost productivity. That's the bill.

11
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.560
Here's the receipt for the fix: Your decentralized D.F.D.A.

12
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:15.220
costs forty million dollars per year to run. It saves fifty-eight point six billion dollars per year in clinical trial waste.

13
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.620
That's six hundred and thirty-seven to one returns on R. and D.

14
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:27.600
savings alone. TOTAL SOCIETAL BENEFIT (including lives saved): eighty-four point eight million to one.

15
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.860
I know. Those numbers look like someone fell asleep on the keyboard.

16
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:39.220
They didn't. Here's the breakdown. The Math: From forty-one thousand dollars to five hundred dollars.

17
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.520
Traditional Phase Three trials cost forty-one thousand dollars per patient.

18
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:51.960
That's a Tesla. Per person. To find out if a pill works. The pill costs thirty-seven cents to make.

19
00:01:52.460 --> 00:02:00.420
The paperwork weighs more than the patient. Then the Oxford RECOVERY trial proved you can do the same thing for five hundred

20
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:09.600
dollars. During a pandemic. While panicking. They tested COVID treatments on more than forty thousand patients by: Using

21
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.660
existing hospital staff (revolutionary concept: doctors treating patients).

22
00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:20.320
Collecting data electronically (instead of sacrificing forests to the paperwork gods).

23
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.600
Focusing on what actually matters: does the patient live or die?

24
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.520
(not "did they complete form twenty-seven B in triplicate?").

25
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.980
Your decentralized D.F.D.A. takes this model global. Eighty to one hundred and sixty times cost reduction.

26
00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:46.380
Same data quality. Better real-world applicability. It turns out you don't need seventeen committees to answer the question

27
00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:52.100
"did the medicine work?". Where the fifty-eight point six billion dollars Comes From.

28
00:02:53.480 --> 00:03:00.720
Global clinical trial spending: sixty billion dollars per year (and growing, because apparently inefficiency scales beautifully).

29
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:09.420
Baseline estimate: ninety-seven point seven percent efficiency gains across the market (based on RECOVERY trial's actual

30
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:17.400
per-patient cost ratio). Ninety-seven point seven percent of sixty billion dollars equals fifty-eight point six billion

31
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:23.420
dollars saved annually. What fifty-eight point six billion dollars Buys You.

32
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:30.000
Each new drug approval is worth an average of one point six two billion dollars in market value.

33
00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:37.860
The D.F.D.A. approves roughly fifty drugs per year. That is the speed at which a species with nuclear weapons and artificial

34
00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:46.860
intelligence has chosen to cure itself. Staggering. With twelve point three times more trial capacity, you could test far

35
00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:54.960
more treatments at once. With the money saved every year, you could: Fund ten thousand new pragmatic clinical trials (at

36
00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.040
five million dollars each, which is still luxurious by RECOVERY standards).

37
00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:08.260
Test treatments for seven thousand rare diseases currently ignored (because orphan diseases aren't profitable enough for

38
00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:16.980
your orphan-making economic system). Cut drug development time from seventeen years to three to five years (most terminal

39
00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.340
patients don't have seventeen years; this has not occurred to anyone in charge).

40
00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:31.780
Make medicines affordable by eliminating two point six billion dollars in development costs (roughly ninety percent of which

41
00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:42.120
is lawyers arguing about commas). Daily Opportunity Cost. Every day without this system costs one hundred and sixty-one

42
00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:51.200
million dollars in wasted trial spending alone. Delayed treatments cost seven point nine four billion disability-adjusted

43
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.660
life years, or D.A.L.Y.s, each year. All because of the regulatory lag period.

44
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.240
The lag period is not a safety feature. It is a waiting room where people die.

45
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:11.160
You just gave the waiting room a nicer name. See the Daily Opportunity Cost of Inaction for calculations.

46
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:20.300
What It Costs to Run. Your decentralized D.F.D.A. costs forty million dollars a year to operate.

47
00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:26.840
Servers, engineers, compliance, global integration. The boring stuff that saves billions.

48
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:35.780
One-time build cost is similar. For context, this is roughly what your species spends annually on Halloween costumes for

49
00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:42.000
dogs. I need you to sit with that for a moment. Less than one fighter jet.

50
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.190
Less than one yacht. Less than what FIFA spends on "hospitality."

51
00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:52.560
See the Decentralized D.F.D.A. Cost-Benefit Analysis for detailed costs.

52
00:05:53.960 --> 00:06:04.640
ROI Scenarios. In a conservative scenario, the R.O.I. is six hundred and thirty-seven to one, counting research and development

53
00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:11.560
